---
title: Static sites on AWS without click-ops
description: S3, CloudFront OAC and GitHub OIDC, wired end-to-end in Terraform.
publishDate: 2026-09-20
tags: [aws, terraform, astro]
draft: false
---

There is a two-checkbox version of hosting a static site on AWS: turn on S3
static website hosting, make the bucket public, point DNS at it. It works, it is
fast to set up, and everything you might later want — HTTPS, a cache, security
headers, a real 404, a deploy that isn't someone's laptop — is bolted on
afterwards or not at all.

The version I keep building instead is a private bucket behind CloudFront, with
GitHub Actions deploying through OIDC, and all of it in Terraform. Both this
site's `infra/` and [taisei-karate](/projects/taisei-karate/)'s are this shape.
Most of it is unremarkable once written down. One part is not — it is the kind
of thing you only learn by having production stop working — so that part is at
the end.

## Why Origin Access Control beats a public bucket

S3's website-hosting mode gives you directory indexes (`/about` → `/about/index.html`)
for free, which is the one genuinely nice thing about it. The price is that the
bucket is readable by anyone who knows its name. Your CloudFront distribution is
then a suggestion: the origin is equally reachable directly, over plain HTTP,
without your cache, your `Strict-Transport-Security` header, your
`Content-Security-Policy`, or your redirect to HTTPS.

Origin Access Control closes that. The bucket blocks all public access and its
policy grants exactly one principal exactly one action:

```hcl
data "aws_iam_policy_document" "bucket" {
  statement {
    principals {
      type        = "Service"
      identifiers = ["cloudfront.amazonaws.com"]
    }
    actions   = ["s3:GetObject"]
    resources = ["${aws_s3_bucket.site.arn}/*"]

    condition {
      test     = "StringEquals"
      variable = "AWS:SourceArn"
      values   = [aws_cloudfront_distribution.site.arn]
    }
  }
}
```

The `SourceArn` condition matters as much as the principal. Without it you have
granted *CloudFront* read access, not *your distribution* — anyone's
distribution could be pointed at your bucket. With it, there is exactly one path
to an object.

CloudFront then signs each origin request with SigV4:

```hcl
resource "aws_cloudfront_origin_access_control" "site" {
  name                              = "${var.project}-oac"
  origin_access_control_origin_type = "s3"
  signing_behavior                  = "always"
  signing_protocol                  = "sigv4"
}
```

Two consequences are worth knowing before you commit to this.

**You lose directory indexes.** S3's REST endpoint has no concept of them — a
request for `/about` is a request for an object literally named `about`, which
does not exist. A CloudFront Function on viewer-request puts them back:

```js
function handler(event) {
    var request = event.request;
    if (request.uri.endsWith('/')) {
        request.uri += 'index.html';
    } else if (!request.uri.includes('.')) {
        request.uri += '/index.html';
    }
    return request;
}
```

**A missing object comes back as 403, not 404.** The policy above grants
`s3:GetObject` and nothing else — no `s3:ListBucket` — so S3 cannot tell you
"not found" without also telling you what does exist, and returns "denied"
instead. Map both codes to your 404 page and stop worrying about which one you
got:

```hcl
custom_error_response {
  error_code            = 403
  response_code         = 404
  response_page_path    = "/404.html"
  error_caching_min_ttl = 0
}
```

## Deploying with OIDC instead of an access key

The other half of click-ops is a long-lived `AKIA…` key pasted into GitHub
Secrets. GitHub Actions can federate with AWS directly: the job gets a
short-lived OIDC token, STS exchanges it for temporary credentials, and no
secret exists to leak or rotate.

Register GitHub's issuer as an IAM identity provider, then write a trust policy
that is narrow in three dimensions at once — issuer, audience, and subject:

```hcl
data "aws_iam_policy_document" "assume" {
  statement {
    actions = ["sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"]

    principals {
      type        = "Federated"
      identifiers = [aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.github.arn]
    }

    condition {
      test     = "StringEquals"
      variable = "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud"
      values   = ["sts.amazonaws.com"]
    }

    condition {
      test     = "StringLike"
      variable = "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub"
      values   = ["repo:${var.github_repo}:ref:refs/heads/main"]
    }
  }
}
```

The `sub` condition is the security boundary. Omit it and *any* GitHub
repository in the world can assume your role. Scope it to one repo and one ref
and only a workflow running on `main` in your repository can deploy.

Keep the deploy role small, too. Uploading a static build needs three things:

```hcl
statement {
  actions   = ["s3:ListBucket"]
  resources = [aws_s3_bucket.site.arn]
}

statement {
  actions   = ["s3:PutObject", "s3:DeleteObject"]
  resources = ["${aws_s3_bucket.site.arn}/*"]
}

statement {
  actions   = ["cloudfront:CreateInvalidation"]
  resources = [aws_cloudfront_distribution.site.arn]
}
```

Terraform itself needs far more than that — IAM, CloudFront, ACM, Route 53 — so
it gets a second role. A deploy job should not be able to rewrite its own trust
policy.

## The subject claim is not always `owner/repo`

Here is the part that will waste your afternoon.

`repo:owner/name:ref:refs/heads/main` is what every tutorial shows, and it is
what GitHub sends — unless your repository uses **immutable subject claims**.
Those build the subject from GitHub's numeric owner and repository IDs as well
as their current names, so a recycled namespace can never resurrect someone
else's trust policy. Repositories created after 15 July 2026 get the format
automatically, and so does any repository renamed or transferred after that
date; existing repositories can opt in.

taisei-karate was created on 25 July 2026 — ten days after that cutover — so it
has carried the immutable subject from its first workflow run, with no rename
and nothing opted into (`use_default: true`). The prefix its tokens actually
carry is not `repo:igor-siergiej/taisei-karate` but:

```
repo:igor-siergiej@79415930/taisei-karate@1312344887
```

— owner login, `@`, owner ID; repository name, `@`, repository ID.


The failure mode is unhelpful. The token is valid, the issuer matches, the
audience matches — the `sub` condition simply doesn't, so
`sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` fails with `Not authorized to perform
sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity`. Nothing in that message mentions repository
names, and re-reading the trust policy tells you nothing because it looks
exactly like the one in the documentation.

You can ask GitHub what it will actually send:

```bash
gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/oidc/customization/sub
```

```json
{
  "use_default": true,
  "use_immutable_subject": true,
  "sub_claim_prefix": "repo:igor-siergiej@79415930/taisei-karate@1312344887"
}
```

`use_immutable_subject: true` is the tell. Feed that prefix into the trust
policy instead of the repository name. Because this is now a fact about a
repository rather than a cosmetic string, it is worth writing down where the
next person will look:

```hcl
variable "github_repo" {
  description = "GitHub's OIDC sub-claim prefix for the repo allowed to assume the deploy role. Normally 'owner/repo', but this repo was renamed at some point, so GitHub now issues tokens with an immutable, ID-suffixed subject instead (see `gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/oidc/customization/sub` -> sub_claim_prefix) — using the plain name here would make every AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity call 'Not authorized'."
  type        = string
  default     = "igor-siergiej@79415930/taisei-karate@1312344887"
}
```

(The variable holds the part after `repo:`, because the trust-policy document
above already builds `"repo:${var.github_repo}:…"` — so drop `repo:` from what
the API hands you.)

That comment is also a small object lesson in its own right: its *explanation* is
wrong. I wrote "this repo was renamed at some point" because a rename was the
story I had in my head, and never checked it. One call to
`gh api repos/igor-siergiej/taisei-karate --jq .created_at` returns
`2026-07-25T22:29:48Z` — after the 15 July cutover — and the sub endpoint above
reports `use_default: true`, so nothing was renamed and nothing was opted into.
The workaround was right and the reason was invented. Write down the value you
verified; be careful about writing down a cause you only assumed.

## While you're in there: `environment:` rewrites the subject too

The same repository turned up a second version of the same bug, which is worth
knowing because it looks identical from the outside.

Adding `environment: production` to a job does not just gate it behind an
approval — it **replaces** the subject claim. Instead of
`repo:<repo>:ref:refs/heads/main` the token carries
`repo:<repo>:environment:production`. Every AWS-touching job in that repository
started targeting a GitHub environment, and every one of them started failing
with the same "Not authorized", diagnosed the same way: read the actual `sub`
out of CloudTrail rather than guessing.

So the trust policy lists the forms that are genuinely in use:

```hcl
values = [
  "repo:${var.github_repo}:ref:refs/heads/main",
  "repo:${var.github_repo}:environment:${local.gh_environment_name}",
]
```

And for the Terraform role, which is also assumed from pull-request-triggered
plan jobs, `repo:<repo>:pull_request` — a third shape that is not a ref at all.

## The shape of the thing

Put together: a private bucket, one distribution allowed to read it, a
viewer-request function restoring directory indexes, 403 and 404 both landing on
a real 404 page, and two OIDC roles — a narrow one that syncs the bucket and
invalidates the cache, and a broader one that runs `terraform plan` on a pull
request and `terraform apply` on merge. No stored AWS credentials, and nothing
that only exists because somebody clicked it once.

The one thing I would tell anyone starting this: when
`AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` says "Not authorized", do not re-read your trust
policy. Go and find the `sub` claim that was actually presented — in CloudTrail,
or from `gh api` — and compare the two strings. The bug is almost always that
they differ in a way the documentation never mentions.
